<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Louis Barthou</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1862">1862</a>–<a href="/years/1934">1934</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Louis Barthou was a French politician who was prime minister in 1913 and foreign minister in 1934. He sought to contain Nazi <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> through alliances in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-europe">Eastern Europe</a>, including with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a>, and was killed on 9 October 1934 in Marseille during the assassination of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-i-of-russia">Alexander I</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yugoslavia">Yugoslavia</a>, struck by a stray police bullet.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/louis-barthou.jpg" alt="Louis Barthou" width="320" height="429" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: George Grantham Bain Collection (Library of Congress) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Louis_Barthou_01.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Barthou was born on 25 August 1862 in Oloron-Sainte-Marie. He served as a deputy for his home constituency and was an authority on trade-union history and law. He held ministerial office on 13 occasions besides his premiership, and was Foreign Minister in 1917 and 1934. As a national hero of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">World War I</a> and a recognised author, he was elected to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/academie-francaise">Académie française</a> at the end of that war. He loved the arts and worked with their leaders to publicise their fields, and the arts community dubbed him the minister of poets.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">In office</h2><p>Barthou was prime minister from 22 March to 9 December 1913. His time in office saw a law of June 1913 safeguarding women workers before and after childbirth and, in July, the introduction of allowances for families with children. As foreign minister in 1934 he tried to create an Eastern Pact of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/czechoslovakia">Czechoslovakia</a> and the Baltic states, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> in some proposals, resting on French guarantees of the Soviet Union's borders and Soviet guarantees of Nazi Germany's eastern borders. In September 1934 he obtained the Soviet Union's entry into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/league-of-nations">League of Nations</a>.</p><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>'s withdrawal from the League he began a rearmament programme that focused first on the Navy and the Air Force. He was the primary figure behind the Franco-Soviet Treaty of Mutual Assistance of 1935, which his successor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pierre-laval">Pierre Laval</a> signed.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Barthou met King Alexander I of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yugoslavia">Yugoslavia</a> during his state visit to Marseille in October 1934. On 9 October Vlado Chernozemski, a Bulgarian, shot the king, and another bullet struck Barthou in the arm and severed an artery; he died of blood loss within an hour. The plot had been planned in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> in August 1934 by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ante-pavelic">Ante Pavelić</a>, head of the Croatian Ustaše. A ballistic report of 1935, made public only in 1974, found that Barthou had been hit by an 8 mm Modèle 1892 revolver round of a kind used by French police, so he was killed by the police response rather than by the assassin. He was given a state funeral four days later.</p><p>The two deaths led to the Convention for the Prevention and Punishment of Terrorism, concluded at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/geneva">Geneva</a> on 16 November 1937, signed by 25 nations and ratified only by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Louis Barthou” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The failure to answer it fed radical movements: the Ustaše, founded by Ante Pavelić after 1929, sought Croatian independence through terrorism and, with the Macedonian revolutionary organisation, organised the assassination of King Alexander in Marseille in 1934, in which the French foreign minister <b>Louis Barthou</b> also died.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-yugoslavia">Kingdom of Yugoslavia · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Oloron-Sainte-Marie, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. Died in Marseille, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=43.19,-0.61&amp;year=1908" aria-label="Oloron-Sainte-Marie on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Louis Barthou"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4962 3881h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4962 3881h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=43.19,-0.61&amp;year=1908">What was here in 1908? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
